/*Q3. Write a Java program to demonstrate arrays of objects, passing objects as method arguments, and returning objects from methods.
Class: Student
Create a class named Student with the following instance variables:
• id (int): Stores the student's ID.
• name (String): Stores the student's name.
• marks (double): Stores the student's marks.
Implement the following:
1. A parameterized constructor Student(int id, String name, double marks) that initializes all three instance variables using the this
keyword.
2. A method displayDetails() that prints the student's ID, name, and marks, each on a separate line, using the following format:
ID: <id>
Name: <name>
Marks: <marks>
Class: StudentOperations
Create a class named StudentOperations with the following methods:
• displayStudents(Student[] students): Accepts an array of Student objects and calls displayDetails() for every student in the array.
• findTopper(Student[] students): Accepts an array of Student objects, identifies the student with the highest marks, and returns that
Student object. If multiple students have the same highest marks, return the first such student in the array.
Class: StudentRecord
The StudentRecord class should:
1. Read the number of students.
Dis Create an array of Student objects of the specified size.
3. Read each student's ID, name, and marks, and create a Student object for each student.
4. Create a StudentOperations object and pass the student array to displayStudents().
5. Call findTopper() with the same array and store the returned Student object.
6. Print "Topper Details: " and display the returned student's details using displayDetails().
Input Format:
1. The first line contains an integer N, representing the number of students.
2. The next N lines contain the details of each student in the following format:
• Student ID (int)
• Student Name (String)
• Student Marks (double)
Output Format:
• Display the details of all students in the following format:
ID: <id>
Name: <name>
Marks: <marks>
• After displaying all students, print:
Topper Details:
ID: <id>
Name: <name>
Marks: <marks>
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;
    double marks;
 
    Student(int id, String name, double marks) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }
 
    void displayDetails() {
        System.out.println("ID: " + id);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Marks: " + marks);
    }
}
 
class StudentOperations {
    void displayStudents(Student[] students) {
        for (Student s : students) {
            s.displayDetails();
        }
    }
 
    Student findTopper(Student[] students) {
        Student top = students[0];
        for (Student s : students) {
            if (s.marks > top.marks) top = s;   // first student wins on a tie
        }
        return top;
    }
}
 
public class StudentRecord {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        Student[] students = new Student[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int id = sc.nextInt();
            sc.nextLine();
            String name = sc.nextLine().trim();
            double marks = sc.nextDouble();
            students[i] = new Student(id, name, marks);
        }
        StudentOperations ops = new StudentOperations();
        ops.displayStudents(students);
        Student topper = ops.findTopper(students);
        System.out.println("Topper Details:");
        topper.displayDetails();
        sc.close();
    }
}
